  "slug": "behavior-darwin-gene-glrx2-ce39",
  "summary": "Recent preprints and one published study link organelle remodeling, mitochondrial redox genetics and mtDNA heteroplasmy detection.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is mitochondrial and organelle architecture as a readout of cell state. In living adult C. elegans, MoMA maps six organelles across somatic cells and reports an abrupt midlife architectural state shift. A separate C. elegans ultrastructural study shows that mitochondria remodel during germ cell differentiation and oxidative stress, forming fzo-1-dependent protective morphotypes. At the genetic level, a frameshift deletion disabling the mitochondrial redox enzyme GLRX2 underlies somersaulting in domestic pigeons, with mouse and zebrafish models addressing the phenotype. Single-molecule FISH protocols for mtDNA variants, deletions and haplotypes in human and Drosophila cells extend this framework to quantifying heteroplasmy. The published FLP recombinase study does not share this thread directly; it reports that high multicopy FLP expression disrupts synaptic protein trafficking in C. elegans, a separate caution for transgenic tool design.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-23"
}
